{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The failures occurred while the hand was already at the button's timing hover, waiting to stop the oscillating cube on the requested second, third, fourth or fifth target passage. The unchanged motion_reference contained the initial endpoint and first elapsed-motion frames with absolute step labels, sufficient to infer periods of 120 or 240 steps and the requested passage time. The policy nevertheless waited four or five steps when descent was due, or made the final wait four or five steps too long. This breaks SUFFICIENCY in the action instruction: its local activation-lead table omitted the observed button geometries, so the nominal uncalibrated lead of 20 steps produced a late press-start time. It is not a memory-content or memory-transition failure.

Extend the existing spatial calibration rule for descents from a timing hover near z=0.15–0.156 m to a button-centered commanded z=0.0075 m. Add L=24 control steps near button xy=(-0.2320, -0.1128) m and near (-0.2236, -0.1452) m. Add L=25 control steps near (-0.1930, -0.0965) m, near (-0.1775, -0.0937) m, and near (-0.1776, 0.0342) m. These are local geometry calibrations, selected using the localized button center, not the achieved hover xy, cube color, requested ordinal or period. Preserve the earlier calibration anchors; do not extrapolate these additions into an exact formula for arbitrary button locations. The nominal 20-step fallback must not override an applicable local calibration.

Keep the existing passage-time and scheduling rules: T_N=P/4+(N-1)P/2, S=T_N-L, and t equal to reported absolute elapsed_control_steps. While at the timing hover, press immediately when t>=S-1; otherwise wait min(30, round(S-t)) steps. Use the button-centered press waypoint with z=0.0075, orientation [-3.1416, 0, 0], and gripper command -1. The new calibrations yield press starts of 186 for a fourth passage at 210, 66 for a second passage at 90, 245 for a fifth passage at 270, 275 for a third passage at 300, and 155 for a second passage at 180. L remains the effective activation lead, not the full descent duration. No other action rule needs removal or replacement."
}

